From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000021 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: sf2000-linux <sf2000-linux@example.invalid>
Date: Mon, 11 May 2026 14:20:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] mips: sf2000: enter first TLBWI directly

The physical unit still reaches the "wired skip/read" diagnostic and then
does not show the following marker. At this point the following generated code
is another diagnostic call before any TLB write, so remove that call boundary
from the SF2000 early flush path. The next visible TLB marker now means the
first indexed TLB write has completed.

Also use low kuseg VPN2 values for the SF2000 invalid entries instead of
CKSEG0 VPN2 values. These entries have invalid EntryLo values either way, but
low VPN2 values avoid relying on this core accepting TLB EntryHi values from an
unmapped segment during the bring-up flush.
---
 arch/mips/mm/tlb-r4k.c | 3 +--
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/mips/mm/tlb-r4k.c b/arch/mips/mm/tlb-r4k.c
index be2a375cc5dc..a727fbe8376a 100644
--- a/arch/mips/mm/tlb-r4k.c
+++ b/arch/mips/mm/tlb-r4k.c
@@ -184,9 +184,8 @@ void local_flush_tlb_all(void)
 	sf2000_tlb_tick("tlb-flush-after-wired-read");
 
 	if (IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_MIPS_SF2000)) {
-		sf2000_tlb_tick("tlb-flush-before-first-write");
 		while (entry < 16) {
-			write_c0_entryhi(CKSEG0 + (entry << (PAGE_SHIFT + 1)));
+			write_c0_entryhi(entry << (PAGE_SHIFT + 1));
 			write_c0_index(entry);
 			write_c0_entrylo0(0);
 			write_c0_entrylo1(0);
-- 
2.50.1
